        <p>Chart week is the Billboard Hot 100 week that contains October 24 that year.</p>
        <p>Week of run says which week of the song's time at number one that was, such as 2 of 5.</p>
        <p>Chart weeks and weeks at number one come from this site's weekly Hot 100 file, and the story lines are credited in Sources.</p>

        <h2 id="answer">The Short Answer: Who Was on Top Each Year</h2>
        <p>For this week in music history, the Hot 100 chart week that includes October 24, the number one song was a different record in every year from 1970 to 1979.</p>
        <p>Here is what the ten weeks show at a glance:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>The ten songs spent {{TOTAL_WEEKS}} weeks at number one in total.</li>
          <li>"You Light Up My Life" had the longest run, with ten weeks starting October 15, 1977.</li>
          <li>{{N_FIRST}} of the ten songs were in their first week at number one that week.</li>
          <li>{{N_MID}} were somewhere in the middle of a longer run, and "Kiss You All Over" was in the last week of its four.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Want Your Own Week?</h3>
        <p>The <a href="/pages/birthday-number-one">Birthday #1 Song Finder</a> does this for any 1970s birthday.</p>
        <p>Enter the date and it shows the song that was on top that week.</p>

        <h2 id="method">How the October 24 Week Was Picked</h2>
        <p>Billboard chart weeks do not line up with the calendar, so there is no single week called October 24.</p>
        <p>This page takes the weekly Hot 100 week that contains October 24 in each year.</p>
        <p>That week began on a different date each year:</p>
        <ul>
{{DATE_LIST}}
        </ul>

        <h3>Where the Numbers Come From</h3>
        <p>Chart weeks and weeks at number one come from the weekly Hot 100 file on this site.</p>
        <p>The story line for each song is a fact from that song's Wikipedia page.</p>
        <p>Anything that could not be confirmed against those sources was left out.</p>

        <h2 id="early">This Week in Music History, 1970 to 1972: A Family Act, a Singer and a Live Novelty</h2>
        <p>The first three Octobers of the decade gave three very different sounds.</p>

        <h3>1970: "I'll Be There" by the Jackson 5</h3>
        <p>"I'll Be There" was the Jackson 5's fourth number one in a row, after "I Want You Back," "ABC" and "The Love You Save."</p>
        <p>Wikipedia credits Berry Gordy, Hal Davis, Bob West and Willie Hutch as its writers.</p>
        <p>It ran five weeks at number one, from October 17 to November 14, and October 24 was the second.</p>
        <p>The full story is on the <a href="/blog/songs/ill-be-there-jackson-5/">I'll Be There Jackson 5</a> page.</p>
        <p>More of their hits are collected on <a href="/blog/artists/jackson-5-songs/">Jackson 5 Songs</a>.</p>

        <h3>1971: "Maggie May" by Rod Stewart</h3>
        <p>"Maggie May" started life as the B-side of "Reason to Believe."</p>
        <p>Radio stations flipped the single and played "Maggie May" instead, and it became the bigger side.</p>
        <p>Rod Stewart wrote it with Martin Quittenton.</p>
        <p>It spent five weeks at number one in the United States, and October 23 was the fourth.</p>

        <h3>1972: "My Ding-a-Ling" by Chuck Berry</h3>
        <p>Chuck Berry's one number one on the Hot 100 was a live recording.</p>
        <p>It was captured on February 3, 1972, at the Lanchester Arts Festival in Coventry, England.</p>
        <p>The song itself is older than that night, since Dave Bartholomew wrote and first recorded it in 1952.</p>
        <p>It topped the chart for two weeks, and October 21 was the first.</p>

        <h2 id="middle">This Week in Music History, 1973 to 1975: Two One-Week Wonders and a Backing Vocal</h2>
        <p>The middle of the decade moved faster, with two songs that held the top for a single week.</p>

        <h3>1973: "Angie" by the Rolling Stones</h3>
        <p>"Angie" came out on August 20, 1973, as the lead single from Goats Head Soup.</p>
        <p>The Stones recorded the album at Dynamic Sounds Studio in Kingston, Jamaica, in November and December 1972.</p>
        <p>The credit reads Jagger and Richards, though Keith Richards later said the basic melody and the title were his.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia calls it the band's seventh and next-to-last Hot 100 number one, ahead of "Miss You" in 1978.</p>
        <p>It spent one week on top, starting October 20.</p>

        <h3>1974: "Nothing From Nothing" by Billy Preston</h3>
        <p>Billy Preston wrote "Nothing From Nothing" with Bruce Fisher.</p>
        <p>It came from his album The Kids & Me and was released as a single on August 5, 1974.</p>
        <p>It was his second solo number one, after "Will It Go Round in Circles" in 1973.</p>
        <p>It held the top for one week, starting October 19.</p>

        <h3>1975: "Bad Blood" by Neil Sedaka</h3>
        <p>Neil Sedaka wrote "Bad Blood" with Phil Cody.</p>
        <p>Elton John sings uncredited backing vocals on it.</p>
        <p>The record stayed on top for three weeks, and the week of October 18 was the second.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia calls it the most successful single of Sedaka's career.</p>
        <p>If you like the Elton John connection, <a href="/blog/artists/elton-john/">Elton John</a> has his own page here.</p>

        <h2 id="late">This Week in Music History, 1976 to 1979: Ballads, a Record and an Instrumental</h2>
        <p>The last four Octobers brought the decade's biggest ballad run and a surprise at the end.</p>

        <h3>1976: "If You Leave Me Now" by Chicago</h3>
        <p>"If You Leave Me Now" was the first number one for Chicago.</p>
        <p>Peter Cetera wrote it and sang lead, and the band took it to the top on October 23, 1976.</p>
        <p>It stayed two weeks in the United States and three in the United Kingdom.</p>
        <p>It also won two Grammys, including Best Pop Vocal Performance by a Duo, Group or Chorus.</p>
        <p>The whole story is on <a href="/blog/songs/if-you-leave-me-now/">If You Leave Me Now</a>.</p>

        <h3>1977: "You Light Up My Life" by Debby Boone</h3>
        <p>"You Light Up My Life" ran ten weeks at number one, from October 15 to December 17, 1977.</p>
        <p>Wikipedia says that set a new Hot 100 record for most weeks at number one.</p>
        <p>Joseph Brooks wrote it, and Kasey Cisyk first recorded it for the film's soundtrack.</p>
        <p>Brooks won a Grammy for Song of the Year and an Academy Award for Best Original Song.</p>
        <p>The song is number 3 on the 1978 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>

        <h3>1978: "Kiss You All Over" by Exile</h3>
        <p>Mike Chapman and Nicky Chinn wrote "Kiss You All Over," and Chapman produced it.</p>
        <p>Chapman said he was inspired by Barry White's "It's Ecstasy When You Lay Down Next to Me."</p>
        <p>The strings on the record are played on a synthesizer.</p>
        <p>It held number one for four weeks from September 30, and October 21 was the last of them.</p>

        <h3>1979: "Rise" by Herb Alpert</h3>
        <p>"Rise" is an instrumental, written by Herb Alpert's nephew Randy "Badazz" Alpert with Andy Armer.</p>
        <p>It was the title track of Alpert's 1979 album Rise.</p>
        <p>It spent two weeks at number one, starting October 20.</p>
        <p>Alpert had topped the Hot 100 before, in 1968, with a vocal, "This Guy's in Love with You."</p>
        <p>The song is number 80 on the 1979 year-end Hot 100, in this site's year-end data.</p>

